    part st162 : Statement {
        :>> id = "ea52c2a9-77e6-4571-9bf8-349ab460058d";
        :>> title = "GH#90 [bug] parser-coverage baseline ships at the engine's own census with no headroom for the adopter, so migrate reverts on its own output";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "The resync ships `parser-coverage-baseline.toml` with `skipped = 7`, which is the engine's own census with no headroom for the adopting project. Any project carrying even one skipped statement of its own goes red the instant migrate installs the baseline, so `keel migrate` reverts on its own output.\n\nSecond of two blockers I hit on the same migrate run; the other is the `charteredBy` path mismatch filed alongside this. Both must clear before a migration can land.\n\n## What happens\n\n`keel migrate` writes, then its post-write gate fails:\n\n```\nERROR skipped-statement count 8 EXCEEDS the committed baseline 7 — a new\n      statement class became invisible to every guard and view (issue231\n      ratchet). Either teach keel-parser the construct, or raise the baseline\n      IN THE SAME COMMIT with the reason (a visible diff, never silent growth).\n[guard:parser-coverage] FAIL — 596 scanned, 1 warning(s), 1 violation(s)\n```\n\n## Cause\n\nThe shipped baseline documents itself as the engine's own legacy set:\n\n`.engine/contracts/parser-coverage-baseline.toml`\n\n```toml\n# Current census: 7 succession statements (`first X then Y` forms the parser reads for ordering\n# but records as skipped in workflow files) - the known, bounded legacy set.\nskipped = 7\n```\n\nThe guard counts skipped statements across the whole tree — `.tracking/` and `.engine/` together — and compares that total against the baseline: `members/keel-guards/src/identity.rs:1127`.\n\nSo the arithmetic in an adopting project is the engine's 7 plus whatever the project contributes. Measured in mine:\n\n- Before migrate, with no baseline file present, the guard reports a warning and passes: `3 statement(s) across 177 file(s) are SKIPPED ... srLocalJamaToolSurface×1, srReviewFragmentFidelity×1, srUpstreamGapIdentification×1`. All three are the project's own `.tracking` items, none are successions, and the old engine contributes none.\n- At the commit migrate actually ran against, one of those three was committed, so the project contributed exactly 1.\n- Post-migrate the count is 8: the engine's 7 successions plus that 1. Baseline 7. Red.\n\nA baseline of exactly the engine's own census means the ratchet is satisfiable only by a project with zero skipped statements of its own. That is not a property any adopter can rely on, and the number of adopter statements the parser skips is not something the adopter chose.\n\n## Why the suggested remedies do not fit\n\nThe message names two, and the mismatch is that both address a *project* that grew a skipped class, not a *resync* that imported seven:\n\n- *Teach keel-parser the construct* — the 7 are the engine's own documented legacy successions in its own workflow files. An adopter cannot fix the engine's parser coverage as a precondition of adopting it.\n- *Raise the baseline in the same commit with the reason* — the file does not exist in the project until migrate writes it, and migrate writes `7`. To raise it the adopter would have to edit a file the resync just authored, inside a run that is atomic and reverts, then commit that edit as though the reason were theirs. The reason is not theirs; the 7 arrived with the engine.\n\n## Repro\n\n1. A project scaffolded from an older vintage, with at least one skipped statement in its own `.tracking/` files and no `.engine/contracts/parser-coverage-baseline.toml`.\n2. `keel gate guard parser-coverage` — passes with a warning naming the project's skipped statements.\n3. Commit or stash so `.tracking/` and `.engine/` are clean, then `keel migrate`.\n4. `parser-coverage` goes red at `project count + 7 > 7`, and the run rolls back.\n\nReproduced on `c592ef6+dirty`, `af5def6` and `ebac62f+dirty`; `.keel/update-attempts.toml` records reverts on 2026-09-08, 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-15.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nOptions, roughly in order of how well each preserves what the ratchet is for:\n\n1. **Count and bound the two populations separately.** The engine's skipped statements are the engine's to ratchet down; the adopter's are the adopter's. One number over both means each side's number moves under the other.\n2. **Have the resync write the baseline at the observed post-write count**, with the reason stated in the file — the resync is the actor that made it necessary, which is exactly what the file's own instructions ask for, and it produces the visible diff the comment wants.\n3. **Scope the baseline to `.engine/` only**, and let a separate adopter-side baseline cover `.tracking/`.\n\nOption 1 or 2 keeps the control's intent, which is that new invisibility is loud. Option 3 is the smallest change but leaves adopter-side growth unbounded.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/90";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st163 : Statement {
        :>> id = "801cee4b-9d00-40a8-936d-928b13d4be54";
        :>> title = "GH#89 [bug] activation-manifest resolves charteredBy against .engine/decisions/ but the resync deploys engine decisions to .engine/reference/decisions/ - migrate can never land downstream";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "`activation-manifest` resolves `charteredBy` against `.engine/decisions/`, but the resync deploys engine decisions to `.engine/reference/decisions/`. In an adopting project the charter can therefore never resolve, and `keel migrate` reverts on its own output.\n\nSame class as #88: engine-shipped content validated against a path layout that only holds in this repository. Also blocks migrate, like #87.\n\n## What happens\n\n`keel migrate` in an adopting project writes 681 files, then its post-write gate fails and the whole run rolls back:\n\n```\nERROR activation.toml: charteredBy = ''d0226'' does not resolve - no\n      .engine/decisions/0226-*.sysml in this project, so the process set is\n      NOT chartered here (issue380/GH#56); re-run project-onboarding or\n      restore your own activation.toml\n[guard:activation-manifest] FAIL — 34 scanned, 0 warning(s), 1 violation(s)\nREVERTING: verified-green or byte-for-byte as before - there is no third state\nROLLED BACK: 681 written file(s) discarded\n```\n\nThe rollback is correct and clean. The problem is that the condition is unreachable by any action the adopting project can take.\n\n## Cause\n\n`charter_resolves` reads exactly one directory, with no recursion:\n\n`members/keel-model/src/onboard.rs:72`\n\n```rust\npub fn charter_resolves(root: &Path, charter: &str) -> bool {\n    let Some(num) = charter.strip_prefix('d').filter(|n| n.len() == 4 && n.chars().all(|c| c.is_ascii_digit())) else {\n        return false;\n    };\n    let prefix = format!(''{num}-'');\n    std::fs::read_dir(root.join(''.engine'').join(''decisions''))\n        .is_ok_and(|rd| rd.flatten().any(|e| e.file_name().to_string_lossy().starts_with(&prefix) && e.path().extension().is_some_and(|x| x == ''sysml'')))\n}\n```\n\nThe guard that calls it is at `members/keel-guards/src/enforcement.rs:1168`.\n\nBut the `engine-resync` step deploys engine decisions one level deeper. From `keel migrate --dry-run` in the adopting project:\n\n```\n.engine/reference/decisions/0226-keel-charters-its-own-process-set.sysml (1 edit(s))\n    add reference/decisions/0226-keel-charters-its-own-process-set.sysml\n```\n\nand it writes the manifest naming that decision:\n\n```\n.engine/contracts/activation.toml (1 edit(s))\n    add contracts/activation.toml\n```\n\nIn this repository the decisions live at `.engine/decisions/`, so the guard passes here and the mismatch is invisible upstream. In an adopting project `.engine/decisions/` holds *that project's own* decisions, so `read_dir` succeeds and finds no `0226-*.sysml`. The adopting project I hit this in has `0001`, `0002`, `0003` there, all its own.\n\n## Why the suggested remedies do not help\n\nThe error names two. Neither is reachable:\n\n- *Restore your own activation.toml from history* — the project has no `activation.toml` in history. Migrate is what creates it, as the dry-run shows (`add contracts/activation.toml`). There is nothing to restore.\n- *Re-run project-onboarding* — onboarding would have to write a `charteredBy` that resolves under the adopter's layout, which means writing a decision into `.engine/decisions/`. That is the project's own decision namespace, and `0226` would collide with or be mistaken for a project decision.\n\n## Repro\n\n1. Take a project scaffolded from an older engine vintage, with its own decisions in `.engine/decisions/` (mine holds `0001`–`0003`) and no `.engine/contracts/activation.toml`.\n2. Commit or stash so `.tracking/` and `.engine/` are clean.\n3. Run `keel migrate`.\n4. It writes, `activation-manifest` fails on the unresolvable charter, everything rolls back.\n\nReproduced three times, on three binaries: `c592ef6+dirty`, `af5def6`, `ebac62f+dirty`. Recorded in `.keel/update-attempts.toml` on 2026-09-08, 2026-09-14 and 2026-09-15, all `outcome = ''reverted''`, all `gate = ''guard''`.\n\n## Suggested fix\n\nResolve the charter wherever the resync actually puts engine decisions. Either search `.engine/reference/decisions/` in addition to `.engine/decisions/`, or have `charter_resolves` take the same path the resync writes to, so the two cannot drift again. A test that runs the guard against a *deployed* tree rather than this repository's tree would have caught it — the current `charter_must_resolve.rs` and `activation_round_trip_loses_nothing.rs` both exercise the authoring layout.\n\nWorth considering alongside #88, since the underlying defect is the same: a guard reading engine-shipped facts at authoring paths rather than deployed paths.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/89";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st164 : Statement {
        :>> id = "ba5d754d-199d-4928-a3fb-e64b1d52b69c";
        :>> title = "GH#88 [control defect] guard:source-reference resolves ENGINE-shipped citations against the ADOPTER's workspace, so the resync can never pass downstream";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "`guard:source-reference` (issue591) scans ENGINE-SHIPPED files and resolves their `file:line` citations against the ADOPTING project's workspace members. Those citations point at keel's own source, which no downstream project has, so the guard can never be satisfied there — and because `keel migrate` gates on the post-apply tree, it makes the engine resync permanently unreachable for any downstream project that happens to be a Rust workspace.\n\nBinary `keel 0.4.1`, build `0c131b7`. Observed on plotwright.\n\n## The two violations\n\n```\n$ keel gate guard source-reference .\nERROR .engine/processes/project-migration.sysml:15: cites `members/keel-process/src/migrate.rs:883-886`,\n      which resolves to no source file under a workspace member's src/ (issue591)\nERROR .engine/skills/project-migration/SKILL.md:10: cites `members/keel-process/src/migrate.rs:883-886`,\n      which resolves to no source file under a workspace member's src/ (issue591)\n[guard:source-reference] FAIL — 2 scanned, 0 warning(s), 2 violation(s)\n```\n\nBoth files are shipped by the engine. The citation in context is engine prose about the engine's own implementation:\n\n```\n// holding `keel-cli/Cargo.toml` as a self-build (migrate.rs:664-666), so THE ENGINE CANNOT MIGRATE\n```\n\n`members/keel-process/src/migrate.rs` is keel's workspace layout. The adopting project is a single `[package]` named `plotwright` with no `members/` directory and no `migrate.rs` — and should not have one. It is a murder-mystery plot tool.\n\n## Why it is invisible on a fresh tree\n\nA bare `keel init` passes, which is why this does not show up in the engine's own testing:\n\n```\n$ keel init . && keel gate guard source-reference .\n[guard:source-reference] PASS — 0 scanned, 0 warning(s), 0 violation(s)\n```\n\n**0 scanned.** The scaffold has no Cargo workspace, so the guard never activates. It only fires once the adopter is itself a Rust project — at which point it immediately fails on files the adopter did not write and cannot fix.\n\n## Why it is worse than a false positive\n\n`keel migrate` gates the post-apply tree and reverts on red (D0336). So:\n\n1. The resync rewrites `.engine/processes/project-migration.sysml` with the citation still in it — the engine ships it that way.\n2. The post-apply gate runs `source-reference`, which fails on that very file.\n3. 589 files roll back.\n\nThe adopter cannot fix it either, because `.engine` is the tool: any local edit is overwritten by the next resync, and the resync is the thing being blocked. On this project it is now the **last** blocker — every other post-apply failure has been worked through (untriaged auto-obligations, `sprint-closure`, and the project-owned `cli-reference` citations in `CLAUDE.md` and the two surviving contracts), and this is what remains.\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\n1. **Do not scan engine-shipped files for source citations at all.** `.engine/` prose describes the engine's implementation by construction; resolving it against the adopter's tree is a category error. Scan `.tracking/` and the project's own docs.\n2. Failing that, **resolve an `.engine/` citation against the engine's repo, not the adopter's**, or recognise a `members/…` prefix as engine-internal and skip it.\n3. Either way, the guard should distinguish ''cites a path in a tree it cannot see'' from ''cites a path that should exist here and does not''. Only the second is a defect in the project being gated.\n\nWorth noting the shape this shares with #68 and #87: three separate post-apply guards have now each independently made the resync unreachable, and in each case the failing condition was something the apply did not introduce and the adopter cannot remove. The pattern may be worth addressing directly — a post-apply gate that fails on anything other than a regression the apply caused will keep finding new ways to block.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/88";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st165 : Statement {
        :>> id = "1866ba33-e516-4d8b-b402-c3eeb37d2e4d";
        :>> title = "GH#87 [bug] migrate is gated on the auto-obligation its own staleness produces, and the rollback DELETES that engine-authored record";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "Two defects from one `keel migrate` run on plotwright, binary `keel 0.4.1` build `0c131b7`, project `.engine` at `b171cd7`. They compound: the first makes the resync unreachable in normal operation, and the second destroys evidence on the way out.\n\n## 1. The resync is gated on a condition the staleness itself keeps producing\n\nThe run rolled back with:\n\n```\nlast update attempt: 0.4.1 on 2026-09-18 REVERTED - guard failed:\n  ERROR obligation35c54864: untriaged — no #Resolves edge (D0077; link a resolving action or Decision)\n```\n\n`obligation35c54864` is not project content. It is an **engine-authored obligation** (D0176/K7), written automatically when the Stop hook's second red pass yields. Trace the cycle:\n\n1. `.engine` is stale, so `keel gate guard` is red (here: `cli-reference` 162, `cli-surface-declared` 133, `viewpoint-renderer` 14, `claude-surface-drift` 5).\n2. The turn gate cannot reach honest state, so the Stop hook yields and the engine records an obligation.\n3. A fresh obligation is untriaged by definition — nothing has resolved it yet.\n4. `keel migrate` gates on the post-apply tree, `guard:issues` fails on the untriaged obligation, and the whole resync reverts.\n5. `.engine` is still stale. Go to 1.\n\nSo the repair for the staleness is blocked by a record the staleness causes. On a project where an agent is working turn by turn, the window in which no untriaged obligation exists is a race — it lasts until the next red turn ends.\n\nThis is a distinct mechanism from #68 (ownership), and it bites *after* #68 is worked around. On this project both have now been hit in sequence: the first two attempts rolled back on `guard:ownership`, this one on an untriaged auto-obligation.\n\n**Suggested fix.** An engine-authored obligation should not gate the migration that would remove its cause. Either exclude auto-recorded obligations from the gate `migrate` runs, or — better — do not treat an obligation recorded *after* the pre-migrate baseline as a regression the apply introduced. The apply did not create it, and reverting 591 files cannot fix it.\n\nRelated: migrate already reasons about exactly these records on the way in, so the concept exists —\n\n```\ntolerated 1 uncommitted engine-authored obligation record(s) — additive, and\nseparable from migration state, so they do not make this tree ambiguous (issue324):\n  ?? .tracking/obligations/red-yield-35c54864.sysml\n```\n\nIt tolerates the record as separable at the start, then lets that same record fail the gate at the end. The two halves disagree.\n\n## 2. The rollback deletes the engine-authored obligation it declared separable\n\nWorse, and this is the part I would treat as the higher severity: **the file is gone.**\n\n```\n$ ls .tracking/obligations/\nred-yield-03358a97.sysml\nred-yield-f50f004c.sysml\n```\n\n`red-yield-35c54864.sysml` no longer exists, and `git status` does not list it as deleted because it was never committed. The rollback restored `.tracking/` wholesale to the pre-apply commit, which discarded an untracked file that migrate had, in its own words, just declared ''additive, and separable from migration state''.\n\nThe consequence is not cosmetic. The obligation was the record that a turn gate yielded while red — the engine's own audit trail of a control being bypassed, which D0176/K7 exists to capture precisely so it cannot be silent. The rollback erased it. `guard:issues` then reports PASS, and it reports PASS *because the evidence was destroyed*, not because anything was resolved.\n\nThere is also a self-defeating quality to it: the record whose untriaged state caused the revert was deleted by that same revert, so re-running migrate immediately afterwards would have succeeded — but only by having lost the fact.\n\n**Suggested fix.** If a file is separable enough to tolerate on the way in, it is separable enough to preserve on the way out. The rollback should restore only paths the apply actually wrote, rather than resetting `.tracking/` and `.engine/` to a commit; or it should stash and replace the tolerated records explicitly.\n\n## Why together\n\nEach alone is bad. Together they form a trap with a quiet exit: the migration fails because of a record, deletes that record, and reports a green `issues` guard afterwards. An operator who re-runs and succeeds would have no indication that a control-bypass record was silently dropped, and the `REVERTED` note in `keel show status` names the obligation by an id that no longer resolves to anything in the tree.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/87";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

    part st166 : Statement {
        :>> id = "dd9507cc-b286-4828-8d68-93c4250df50c";
        :>> title = "GH#86 [bug] record gate-result writes VerdictKind::proposed into a tree whose schema lacks it - the write API produces a file validate rejects, and it blocks all sprint ceremony";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> createdBy = "claudeFable5";
        :>> text = "`keel record gate-result` writes `VerdictKind::proposed` into a project whose `.engine/` schema does not declare that member, and `keel gate validate` then rejects the file the write API just produced. The write path is not vintage-aware: it emits values from the binary's own schema without checking what the project's engine copy can hold.\n\nBinary: `keel 0.4.1`, build `444333d`. Project `.engine/` vintage: build `b171cd7`.\n\n## Sequence\n\n```\n$ keel record gate-result --file .tracking/delivery/sprint4_...sysml /\n    --gate castPortsAndBeatNodesRefineGate --sha d80f13f --verdict pass /\n    --judged-by claudeOpus5 --judged-at 2026-09-18 --evidence-from refine-evidence.md\n143d24f9-0065-4fed-b113-27288ed89705\nnote: recorded proposed - an AI-judged pass on an examined method (demo/analyze/inspect) with no\n      replayable receipt is a proposal until a human judges it, and counts as done for nothing\n      meanwhile (D0312 B)\n```\n\nCorrect behaviour on its own terms — D0312 B downgrading an AI-judged `inspect` pass is the engine being right. Then:\n\n```\n$ keel gate validate .\nERROR: .tracking/delivery/sprint4_castPortsAndBeatNodes.sysml:28 — unknown member `proposed` in enum `VerdictKind`\n       hint: valid members: error, fail, inconclusive, pass\nERROR: .tracking/delivery/sprint4_castPortsAndBeatNodes.sysml:31 — unknown member `proposed` in enum `VerdictKind`\n       hint: valid members: error, fail, inconclusive, pass\n17 tracking file(s) validated — 0 parse error(s), 2 semantic diagnostic(s).\n```\n\nThe two schemas, same binary, different trees:\n\n```\n# this project, .engine at b171cd7\nenum def VerdictKind { pass; fail; inconclusive; error; }\n\n# tree freshly keel init-ed by 444333d\nenum def VerdictKind { pass; fail; inconclusive; error; proposed; }\n```\n\n## Why this is worse than an ordinary staleness symptom\n\nIt is not a stale *doc* or a stale command name. The write API actively produced an invalid tree, and it did so while reporting success. There is no verdict the operator chose here: `--verdict pass` was passed, the engine substituted `proposed` by policy, and the substituted value is one the project cannot represent.\n\n**It blocks all sprint ceremony.** Every autonomous gate in this project's sprint records is `method=inspect` or `method=analyze`, which is what D0017 and D0049 prescribe for them. So D0312 B applies to all of them, so every one of them writes `proposed`, so none can be recorded without failing validate. There is no way to run a sprint on this tree.\n\n**And the repair is the one that cannot run.** The fix is `keel migrate`, which on this project rolls back on `guard:ownership` (#68, where I added this project's occurrence). So a project can arrive at a state where the write API only emits invalid data and the only remedy is refused.\n\n## What I did locally, for the record\n\nAdded the single member to `.engine/schema/core/element.sysml`, matching byte-for-byte what the current engine ships:\n\n```\nenum def VerdictKind { pass; fail; inconclusive; error; proposed; }\n```\n\n`keel gate validate` then reports 17 files clean and `guard:ownership` passes. This is editing `.engine/`, which the project's own CLAUDE.md forbids, and it is a stopgap rather than a fix — the resync will make it redundant. I mention it because it is evidence that the gap is exactly one enum member, not a deep incompatibility.\n\n## Suggested fixes\n\n1. **Make the write path vintage-aware**: before emitting an enum value, check the project's schema declares it. If it does not, refuse with a message naming the member and the required engine version, rather than writing a file that will not validate. A refusal an operator can read beats a success they have to discover is a lie.\n2. **Degrade rather than break** where a policy substitution is involved: if D0312 B wants to downgrade a `pass` and the tree cannot hold `proposed`, that is a case for refusing the record and saying why, not for writing the unrepresentable value.\n3. More generally, this is an argument for the binary knowing the tree's vintage explicitly. #81 already notes that nothing is stamped and the vintage is inferred from content; a recorded engine version would let every write path make this check cheaply.\n";
        :>> saidBy = "williamweatherholtz"; :>> saidAt = "2026-09-18"; :>> channel = StatementChannel::github;
        :>> sourceUrl = "https://github.com/williamweatherholtz/keel/issues/86";
        :>> sourceTrust = SourceTrust::untrusted;
    }

